Unit 3 – Queue
 Introduction

A queue is a linear data structure that follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle. The element inserted first is the first element to be removed.

In this program, a queue is implemented using an array. The queue uses two variables:

Front – points to the first element of the queue.
Rear – points to the last element of the queue.
Basic Queue Operations
Insertion – Adds an element at the rear.
Deletion – Removes an element from the front.
isEmpty – Checks whether the queue is empty.
isFull – Checks whether the queue is full.
Display – Displays all queue elements.
1. Queue Using Array
 Problem Statement

Create a queue of size M using an array and perform insertion, deletion, display, and empty/full checking operations.

 Approach
Define a queue using typedef struct queuetype.
Create an array of size M.
Use front to track the first element.
Use rear to track the last element.
Initialize both front and rear to -1.
Insert elements using newel().
Delete elements using delel().
Check whether the queue is empty using isempty().
Check whether the queue is full using isfull().
Display the queue elements using display().
 Structure Used
typedef struct queuetype
{
    int arr[M];
    int front;
    int rear;
} queuetype;
 Modular Functions
Function	Purpose
initqueue()	Initializes front and rear
newel()	Inserts an element into the queue
delel()	Deletes an element from the queue
isempty()	Checks whether the queue is empty
isfull()	Checks whether the queue is full
display()	Displays queue elements
Queue Representation

After inserting 10, 20, 30, 40, 50:

FRONT                         REAR
  ↓                             ↓
[10] [20] [30] [40] [50]

The first element 10 is removed first.

This follows:

FIFO → First In, First Out
 Insertion and Deletion
